---
// The privacy policy. It must say only what is true of the app today; when that changes, update the text
// here and in src/pages/ru/privacy.astro, and the date in src/i18n.ts (LEGAL_EFFECTIVE).
import TextPage from '../layouts/TextPage.astro';
import Contact from '../components/Contact.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { pageHref } from '../i18n';
---

<TextPage
  lang="en"
  page="privacy"
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="What the Collarbell app collects, why, where it is kept, and how to see, correct or delete it."
  dated
>
  <p slot="intro">
    This policy explains in plain words what the Collarbell app and this website collect, why, where it is
    kept and what you can do about it. Collarbell is in early testing, and the policy describes the app as it
    works today.
  </p>

  <h2 id="controller">Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>
    Collarbell is made by an independent developer, a private individual, who is responsible for your data
    (the “controller”). You can reach the developer here:
  </p>
  <Contact lang="en" subject="Collarbell privacy" />

  <h2 id="account">Your account</h2>
  <p>
    You sign in with your e-mail address and a password through Google Firebase Authentication. Firebase keeps
    the password; the developer never sees it. Collarbell’s server stores your Firebase user ID and your e-mail
    address.
  </p>

  <h2 id="your-data">What you add to the app</h2>
  <p>Collarbell stores what you enter, so that it can show it to you and remind you of it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      your pets: name, species, breed, sex, birth date, coat, microchip and other identifiers, and passport
      details;
    </li>
    <li>vaccinations, parasite treatments and other care records;</li>
    <li>vet visits and notes;</li>
    <li>feeding schedules and “Fed” marks;</li>
    <li>supplies and purchases;</li>
    <li>
      photos and documents you upload. The server re-encodes images and removes their metadata, such as the
      place where a photo was taken;
    </li>
    <li>app settings: language and time zone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="sharing">Shared groups</h2>
  <p>
    Pets and their records belong to a group («My home», «Office»). If you invite someone into your group, or join
    someone else’s, everyone in the group sees its pets, records, documents and supplies, and each other’s name and
    e-mail. Marks show who made them, e.g. who fed the cat. A helper sees everything and can only mark things done.
  </p>

  <h2 id="notifications">Reminders</h2>
  <p>
    Push notifications are sent through Firebase Cloud Messaging, a Google service. For this, the server stores
    your device’s push token.
  </p>

  <h2 id="storage">Where your data is kept</h2>
  <p>
    On the developer’s server in Germany (EU), at the hosting provider Contabo. Backups are made every night on
    the same server and kept for 14 days.
  </p>
  <p>
    The server’s web log records each request’s IP address, time and address. It is kept for 14 days, for
    security and troubleshooting.
  </p>
  <p>
    Firebase Authentication, Firebase Cloud Messaging and Gemini (see below) are Google services: Google
    processes the data they receive on its own servers, which may be outside the EU.
  </p>

  <h2 id="support-log">Support log</h2>
  <p>
    To help when something goes wrong, the app tells the server which screens were opened, a few actions (such as
    “Fed”) and the errors you saw, never what you typed. The server also notes each request it gets: the address,
    the answer and the time, without the contents. Only the developer sees this, and it is kept for 7 days.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">Reading photos with AI</h2>
  <p>
    When you choose to read a photo with AI (a passport page, a document from the vet or a product package),
    that photo is sent to Google’s Gemini API. Nothing is sent to AI unless you start a scan yourself.
  </p>
  <div class="notice notice--warning">
    <Icon name="warning" />
    <p>
      During testing, Collarbell uses the free tier of Gemini. Under it, Google may use the content to improve
      its products, and human reviewers may see it. So please don’t scan anything you don’t want to share.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="barcodes">Looking up barcodes</h2>
  <p>
    When you scan a product’s barcode, only the barcode number is sent to Open Pet Food Facts and UPCitemdb, to
    find the product.
  </p>
  <p>
    The barcode and document scanners work on your phone with Google ML Kit. The camera images stay on the phone,
    but ML Kit may send Google technical information: the phone model, the app version and how well the scanner
    worked.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-doesnt-do">What Collarbell doesn’t do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It shows no ads.</li>
    <li>It doesn’t sell your data.</li>
    <li>
      It has no analytics, advertising or tracking tools. Of Firebase, the app uses only Authentication and
      Cloud Messaging.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>This website sets no cookies, has no analytics and loads nothing from other sites.</p>

  <h2 id="retention">How long data is kept</h2>
  <p>
    Until you delete it. Whatever you delete, including your whole account, also leaves the backups within 14
    days.
  </p>
  <p>
    A household you share with others stays with them when you delete your account: the records and feeding marks
    you made there stay, without your name as their author, and what is written in them (for example the owner’s
    details in a pet passport) stays as it is.
  </p>
  <p><a href={pageHref('en', 'deleteAccount')}>How to delete your account</a></p>

  <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    In the app, <strong>More → Download my data</strong> gives you a copy of everything Collarbell keeps about you,
    as a ZIP file that other programs can read, and <strong>More → Delete account</strong> deletes it. You can
    correct your data in the app too. For anything else, write to the developer at the contact address above.
  </p>
  <p>You can also complain to a data protection authority.</p>

  <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
  <p>
    Collarbell is for adults: you must be 18 or older to use it. This is also a condition of the AI service
    (Google Gemini) that reads photos.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>When this policy changes, the new version is published on this page with its date.</p>
</TextPage>
